WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:07.200
&gt;&gt; I'm going to present today

00:00:07.201 --> 00:00:09.015
Compositional Language Modelling

00:00:09.016 --> 00:00:10.950
for Icon-Based Augmentative

00:00:10.951 --> 00:00:13.620
and Alternative Communication.

00:00:13.621 --> 00:00:19.190
The child you see in
the photo has cerebral palsy.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:25.074
He is using an augmentative
and alternative system,

00:00:25.075 --> 00:00:29.710
an AAC system to communicate with

00:00:29.711 --> 00:00:31.990
his surroundings and basically

00:00:31.991 --> 00:00:34.915
to overcome his speech
language impairments.

00:00:34.916 --> 00:00:39.275
There are two types
of AAC systems,

00:00:39.276 --> 00:00:42.525
either text-based or icon-based.

00:00:42.526 --> 00:00:45.850
This child, and the focus
of today's talk will be

00:00:45.851 --> 00:00:49.850
on an icon-based AAC system.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:54.830
The way he does it is
by choosing one icon

00:00:54.831 --> 00:00:59.810
after another on the way
to composing a message.

00:00:59.811 --> 00:01:03.005
For icon-based AAC system,

00:01:03.006 --> 00:01:05.030
there are mainly two approaches.

00:01:05.031 --> 00:01:07.355
It's either you work with

00:01:07.356 --> 00:01:09.920
hierarchical AAC system in which

00:01:09.921 --> 00:01:14.950
a user navigates a tree on
the way to finding their icon.

00:01:14.951 --> 00:01:17.775
Alternatively, they can use

00:01:17.776 --> 00:01:21.410
a colored-coded board
in which groups

00:01:21.411 --> 00:01:25.315
of icons represent
grammatical functionality.

00:01:25.316 --> 00:01:31.050
Say, the group of
red icons represent verbs,

00:01:31.051 --> 00:01:35.310
or those greens
represent prepositions.

00:01:35.311 --> 00:01:39.450
The problem however,
is that when we scale

00:01:39.451 --> 00:01:43.635
up the vocabulary size
of these icon sets,

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:47.325
these approaches fall short.

00:01:47.326 --> 00:01:51.765
In addition, depending on
the selection modality,

00:01:51.766 --> 00:01:56.910
we might accumulate many errors
because the user might

00:01:56.911 --> 00:01:59.460
require to do a cascade

00:01:59.461 --> 00:02:02.655
of choices until they
reach their target.

00:02:02.656 --> 00:02:06.540
Therefore, naturally,
we thought about

00:02:06.541 --> 00:02:11.055
why not creating icon language
model-based AAC system.

00:02:11.056 --> 00:02:13.890
One thing I will
say is that there's

00:02:13.891 --> 00:02:16.410
not much literature in

00:02:16.411 --> 00:02:18.570
the context of icon

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:22.020
and language models to the
best of our knowledge.

00:02:22.021 --> 00:02:24.450
But we did rely on

00:02:24.451 --> 00:02:31.620
an earlier work that tried to
assign semantics for icons.

00:02:31.621 --> 00:02:40.060
One additional thing I will
mention is that I forgot.

00:02:40.061 --> 00:02:44.520
I'm sorry.

00:02:44.521 --> 00:02:47.385
What you'll see is
a unique problem.

00:02:47.386 --> 00:02:49.755
You'll see in a few slides

00:02:49.756 --> 00:02:52.950
that we are well

00:02:52.951 --> 00:02:55.755
familiar with many types
of language models.

00:02:55.756 --> 00:02:58.380
We'll introduce here
a unique problem

00:02:58.381 --> 00:03:03.255
that most of you probably
haven't heard of by now.

00:03:03.256 --> 00:03:07.665
So, back to our language
models of icons.

00:03:07.666 --> 00:03:10.905
Together with our clinical team,

00:03:10.906 --> 00:03:15.480
we've decided to work on
Symbolstix Icon dataset.

00:03:15.481 --> 00:03:17.800
It is used by
communities who are in

00:03:17.801 --> 00:03:20.140
need for icon-based
communication,

00:03:20.141 --> 00:03:23.440
not only cerebral palsy
individuals but

00:03:23.441 --> 00:03:26.875
also people may experience
traumatic brain injury,

00:03:26.876 --> 00:03:29.980
or ALS, or locked-in syndrome.

00:03:29.981 --> 00:03:34.920
It is human curated
containing 34,000 icons,

00:03:34.921 --> 00:03:37.665
5,000 of which are
of single words,

00:03:37.666 --> 00:03:39.420
the rest are phrases

00:03:39.421 --> 00:03:41.760
which we plan on
exploring in the future.

00:03:41.761 --> 00:03:47.880
We ended up using 3,500
unique single words.

00:03:47.881 --> 00:03:50.725
Let's see an example for an icon.

00:03:50.726 --> 00:03:52.660
So, this is an icon,

00:03:52.661 --> 00:03:55.715
and it associated with metadata.

00:03:55.716 --> 00:03:59.010
We can see its name,

00:03:59.011 --> 00:04:01.335
word type, and synonyms.

00:04:01.336 --> 00:04:05.340
Notice that among its
synonyms it also has phrases.

00:04:05.341 --> 00:04:07.935
But more than that, it also has

00:04:07.936 --> 00:04:11.865
a synonym that suggests
that maybe this icon

00:04:11.866 --> 00:04:14.445
represents slightly
a broader concept

00:04:14.446 --> 00:04:15.960
that perhaps you and

00:04:15.961 --> 00:04:20.370
I would think about synonyms
for the the term, "Agree."

00:04:20.371 --> 00:04:25.470
One last thing.

00:04:25.471 --> 00:04:31.190
There is no corpus available
of these icon sets.

00:04:31.191 --> 00:04:34.475
So, then the question becomes,

00:04:34.476 --> 00:04:40.120
how to create language models
for corpusless symbols set.

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:42.970
So, what we know
for sure we don't

00:04:42.971 --> 00:04:45.010
have is an icon for it,

00:04:45.011 --> 00:04:47.260
but what we may have and we know

00:04:47.261 --> 00:04:50.090
about is that we
have this metadata.

00:04:50.091 --> 00:04:53.625
We know about pre-existing
textual corpora,

00:04:53.626 --> 00:04:56.335
and also about another tool

00:04:56.336 --> 00:04:59.255
of word embedding representation.

00:04:59.256 --> 00:05:03.690
So, maybe we can
utilize and incorporate

00:05:03.691 --> 00:05:06.510
these tools to figure out

00:05:06.511 --> 00:05:10.390
how to simulate an icon language.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:14.980
Let's start with
icon representation.

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:19.320
Let's take the example
of the same icon,

00:05:19.321 --> 00:05:22.020
and retrieve its name
and synonyms.

00:05:22.021 --> 00:05:26.590
We'll go to a
pretrained embeddings,

00:05:26.591 --> 00:05:32.115
take all those terms and
tokens representing this icon,

00:05:32.116 --> 00:05:34.950
compose them together, and now,

00:05:34.951 --> 00:05:37.930
we've represented our icon.

00:05:37.931 --> 00:05:40.689
Also, notice that it captures

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:43.610
the concept of the icon

00:05:43.611 --> 00:05:46.520
as opposed to what
we know about agree.

00:05:46.521 --> 00:05:51.305
So, it represents
better this icon,

00:05:51.306 --> 00:05:53.780
and so on and so forth.

00:05:53.781 --> 00:05:56.870
That's how we make
icon embedding.

00:05:56.871 --> 00:06:01.100
So, this is this path.

00:06:01.101 --> 00:06:05.015
Now, we want to incorporate

00:06:05.016 --> 00:06:09.125
these embeddings into
textural dataset

00:06:09.126 --> 00:06:12.520
that will provide us sequences.

00:06:12.590 --> 00:06:15.920
So, say we have
a sentence such as,

00:06:15.921 --> 00:06:18.575
you agree to solve problems.

00:06:18.576 --> 00:06:22.880
We're going to look into
our icon embedding dictionary.

00:06:22.881 --> 00:06:27.620
One by one, we'll
be able to simulate

00:06:27.621 --> 00:06:33.240
our icon language from
this textual dataset.

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:37.370
Now, we have embedding
in sequences and we

00:06:37.371 --> 00:06:40.565
are ready to train
language models on them.

00:06:40.566 --> 00:06:44.780
We have employed
a simple standard Pi torch

00:06:44.781 --> 00:06:47.800
Architecture for
language modelling,

00:06:47.801 --> 00:06:50.490
and then we have performed it in

00:06:50.491 --> 00:06:56.310
a 5-Fold cross-validation manner.

00:06:56.311 --> 00:06:57.630
I'll just remind you,

00:06:57.631 --> 00:07:00.510
we've employed a simple Pi torch

00:07:00.511 --> 00:07:02.550
Standard Architecture for

00:07:02.551 --> 00:07:05.010
training Language Models in

00:07:05.011 --> 00:07:07.770
a 5-Fold cross-validation manner,

00:07:07.771 --> 00:07:10.500
and we took a look at

00:07:10.501 --> 00:07:15.360
three different measurements
to evaluate language models.

00:07:15.361 --> 00:07:17.880
One was mean reciprocal rank.

00:07:17.881 --> 00:07:21.240
So, for each prediction,

00:07:21.241 --> 00:07:23.060
the model produced, we wanted to

00:07:23.061 --> 00:07:25.345
look at the rank of the targets.

00:07:25.346 --> 00:07:30.060
Another metric was
accuracy at one.

00:07:30.061 --> 00:07:32.130
Meaning, let's look at the

00:07:32.131 --> 00:07:34.545
first guess the model had done,

00:07:34.546 --> 00:07:37.645
and see whether
the target was in or not.

00:07:37.646 --> 00:07:40.875
Another metric was
accuracy of 10.

00:07:40.876 --> 00:07:45.135
Looking at the first 10 guesses

00:07:45.136 --> 00:07:48.910
and looking whether
the target was in or not.

00:07:48.911 --> 00:07:50.760
So, now it's time to

00:07:50.761 --> 00:07:56.730
evaluate language model or

00:07:56.731 --> 00:08:00.090
our choices throughout
the process.

00:08:00.091 --> 00:08:03.350
So, the first thing
we're going to focus on,

00:08:03.351 --> 00:08:07.325
and imagine now that you
can see the process.

00:08:07.326 --> 00:08:10.850
I'll just have you all
recall what the process was.

00:08:10.851 --> 00:08:14.750
We had pretrained embeddings
here generating icons,

00:08:14.751 --> 00:08:19.400
and also both of them go
directly to the textual dataset.

00:08:19.401 --> 00:08:21.250
At the bottom,

00:08:21.251 --> 00:08:24.090
now they're being
translated to embeddings.

00:08:24.091 --> 00:08:26.625
Okay? So, we have
an embedding datasets,

00:08:26.626 --> 00:08:27.940
and we are training

00:08:27.941 --> 00:08:30.515
language models on
these embedding dataset.

00:08:30.516 --> 00:08:32.770
So, one experiment
we've conducted is

00:08:32.771 --> 00:08:35.725
basically playing with
the pretrained embeddings.

00:08:35.726 --> 00:08:40.255
We have used glove
containing 400 entries,

00:08:40.256 --> 00:08:42.550
Train on English view award and

00:08:42.551 --> 00:08:45.035
compared it to context to wreck

00:08:45.036 --> 00:08:49.750
that had 160 entries

00:08:49.751 --> 00:08:52.955
train on British
English web-based text.

00:08:52.956 --> 00:08:55.325
For the textual dataset,

00:08:55.326 --> 00:08:58.110
we have used sublinks US,

00:08:58.111 --> 00:09:00.765
which was the best we could do.

00:09:00.766 --> 00:09:03.600
It contains six million
sentences and was

00:09:03.601 --> 00:09:08.160
a good proxy for AAC type
conversation. Not the best one.

00:09:08.161 --> 00:09:11.025
We actually ran
another experiment that had

00:09:11.026 --> 00:09:19.885
a more AAC-oriented corpus
and had its own thing,

00:09:19.886 --> 00:09:24.120
which I cannot elaborate now
because we don't have time.

00:09:24.121 --> 00:09:30.229
Finally, we wanted also to
play with another parameter,

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:34.095
see whether we can incorporate
just the icon embeddings

00:09:34.096 --> 00:09:36.060
without the pre-trained into

00:09:36.061 --> 00:09:39.480
the textual corpus and
learn language models from.

00:09:39.481 --> 00:09:42.890
So, we compared
the pure one condition

00:09:42.891 --> 00:09:45.605
to a non-pure containing both.

00:09:45.606 --> 00:09:49.970
I'm glad your imagination
is working with me.

00:09:49.971 --> 00:09:53.480
So, as far as results go,

00:09:53.481 --> 00:09:57.140
we basically haven't found
meaningful difference

00:09:57.141 --> 00:10:00.440
between the two types
of the pre-embeddings,

00:10:00.441 --> 00:10:02.360
but we did find that there is

00:10:02.361 --> 00:10:06.575
a clear degradation once we
use just the pure icons,

00:10:06.576 --> 00:10:12.660
and we suspect that
this is because using

00:10:12.661 --> 00:10:15.170
just these icons did not allow

00:10:15.171 --> 00:10:19.080
us to have a good coverage
of the dataset.

00:10:19.670 --> 00:10:24.695
I also wanted to provide some
examples for these icons.

00:10:24.696 --> 00:10:27.050
Maybe I'll have a chance to just

00:10:27.051 --> 00:10:29.795
show examples in
the less minutes I

00:10:29.796 --> 00:10:35.610
have here, maybe not.

00:10:35.611 --> 00:10:39.710
I will get into the
conclusion part and say

00:10:39.711 --> 00:10:47.630
that the biggest limitation

00:10:47.631 --> 00:10:51.380
of this approach is
that basically we

00:10:51.381 --> 00:10:56.120
are simulating an icon
language to the best we can.

00:10:56.121 --> 00:10:58.630
But it's still from

00:10:58.631 --> 00:11:03.350
written text that applies
its own constraints,

00:11:03.351 --> 00:11:06.925
and now, it's this,

00:11:06.926 --> 00:11:09.565
the second part of
the future steps.

00:11:09.566 --> 00:11:12.995
We plan on evaluating
our language models on

00:11:12.996 --> 00:11:17.575
real users at the first step
on healthy ones,

00:11:17.576 --> 00:11:18.710
and perhaps, later on,

00:11:18.711 --> 00:11:21.985
on who I need.

00:11:21.986 --> 00:11:24.290
Also, plan to have
representations for

00:11:24.291 --> 00:11:30.145
MultiSense icons and
Multiphrase icons.

00:11:30.146 --> 00:11:33.015
We would like to thank
our clinical team,

00:11:33.016 --> 00:11:36.320
our North-Eastern
team, as well as

00:11:36.321 --> 00:11:41.370
our funders, and that's it.

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:54.160
&gt;&gt; Oh, yeah.

00:11:54.161 --> 00:11:56.020
While we're waiting to get

00:11:56.021 --> 00:11:57.895
the slides back, any questions?

00:11:57.896 --> 00:12:00.960
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:12:00.961 --> 00:12:03.250
So, is there currently
an effort underway

00:12:03.251 --> 00:12:06.080
to collect an icon-based purpose?

00:12:06.081 --> 00:12:14.560
&gt;&gt; We are trying to push for
it because this limitation,

00:12:14.561 --> 00:12:16.990
we do know that there's

00:12:16.991 --> 00:12:19.250
a good chance that
a real icon language would

00:12:19.251 --> 00:12:23.770
not be subjected to
the constraint imposed perhaps,

00:12:23.771 --> 00:12:27.580
by a corpus, and
we're pushing for it.

00:12:27.581 --> 00:12:30.390
But we would like also, yeah.

00:12:30.391 --> 00:12:33.180
There is no one that I know of.

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:37.550
&gt;&gt; Sorry. Could you say
more about how you got

00:12:37.551 --> 00:12:41.550
the AAC appropriate text
corpus? Where was that from?

00:12:41.551 --> 00:12:45.220
&gt;&gt; So, the SymbolStix dataset,

00:12:45.221 --> 00:12:52.740
which I may go back to.

00:12:52.741 --> 00:12:58.565
So, together with
our clinical team,

00:12:58.566 --> 00:13:01.595
we have a license or a contract

00:13:01.596 --> 00:13:06.235
that this is
a commercial company,

00:13:06.236 --> 00:13:08.790
and under the agreement,

00:13:08.791 --> 00:13:18.135
we're allowed to use
their icon set. Maybe I didn't.

00:13:18.136 --> 00:13:21.270
&gt;&gt; I thought you said you had

00:13:21.271 --> 00:13:23.540
a text corpus which would
be like what people will

00:13:23.541 --> 00:13:26.190
say in the ACC situation.
Where did that come from?

00:13:26.191 --> 00:13:27.935
&gt;&gt; Thanks for clarifying it.

00:13:27.936 --> 00:13:33.650
There is actual a research
conducted by Virtanen L,

00:13:33.651 --> 00:13:37.000
and I can also give
you the exact paper

00:13:37.001 --> 00:13:41.930
in which he gathered

00:13:41.931 --> 00:13:46.410
6,000 sentences from Turkers
who were required under

00:13:46.411 --> 00:13:51.380
constraints to produce
AAC-oriented sentences,

00:13:51.381 --> 00:13:54.830
but also evaluate
their peers' sentences.

00:13:54.831 --> 00:13:57.804
But it's only 6,000,

00:13:57.805 --> 00:14:00.530
and it's not enough.

00:14:00.531 --> 00:14:03.350
So, we had to pet it
with additional corpus

00:14:03.351 --> 00:14:07.500
to make more meaningful
results to train it on.

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:13.510
&gt;&gt; Has there been
any research done

00:14:13.511 --> 00:14:18.335
on comprehension of
an icon-based communication?

00:14:18.336 --> 00:14:20.845
&gt;&gt; Well,

00:14:20.846 --> 00:14:26.835
now I'm talking in the
context of electronic usage,

00:14:26.836 --> 00:14:29.305
but we do know about clinicians.

00:14:29.306 --> 00:14:34.870
They have these communication
boards that they point

00:14:34.871 --> 00:14:37.905
to and try to teach

00:14:37.906 --> 00:14:41.510
a child how to communicate
once they have disabilities.

00:14:41.511 --> 00:14:44.345
So, in that regard,
it's clinically

00:14:44.346 --> 00:14:47.300
being deployed or as a practice.

00:14:47.301 --> 00:14:51.080
&gt;&gt; How does that compare to

00:14:51.081 --> 00:14:53.400
a textual comprehension versus

00:14:53.401 --> 00:14:56.250
an icon-based comprehension?

00:14:56.251 --> 00:14:58.200
&gt;&gt; That's a good point.

00:14:58.201 --> 00:15:05.495
Here's something I
should have said.

00:15:05.496 --> 00:15:08.005
Perhaps, it would be useful for

00:15:08.006 --> 00:15:11.680
people who would use
icons would be perhaps,

00:15:11.681 --> 00:15:13.910
illiterate, or children who

00:15:13.911 --> 00:15:17.340
are during their
development stage.

00:15:17.341 --> 00:15:19.390
But perhaps, if you provide

00:15:19.391 --> 00:15:21.210
a good platform that will provide

00:15:21.211 --> 00:15:24.160
good and fast
predictions would not

00:15:24.161 --> 00:15:28.095
require you go through
a long cascade of selections,

00:15:28.096 --> 00:15:31.420
might be even useful
more than text.

00:15:32.410 --> 00:15:36.935
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:15:36.936 --> 00:15:40.225
&gt;&gt; I was wondering how
polysemous the icons

00:15:40.226 --> 00:15:43.600
are compared to
their corresponding words?

00:15:43.601 --> 00:15:46.950
Or do you try and present
a more specific type

00:15:46.951 --> 00:15:51.130
of sense and metaphor?

00:15:51.131 --> 00:15:52.730
But the other part is,

00:15:52.731 --> 00:15:56.590
Context vec versus
GloVe embeddings,

00:15:56.591 --> 00:15:58.070
did you notice any difference in

00:15:58.071 --> 00:16:00.790
your performance on that?

00:16:00.791 --> 00:16:04.090
If I got what you were
saying correctly.

00:16:04.091 --> 00:16:05.895
&gt;&gt; So, for the first part,

00:16:05.896 --> 00:16:09.330
you're talking about
multi-sense disambiguation

00:16:09.331 --> 00:16:11.935
or representation of icons.

00:16:11.936 --> 00:16:15.300
We do have in
the set different icons

00:16:15.301 --> 00:16:17.300
representing the same term but

00:16:17.301 --> 00:16:20.650
[inaudible] one is a verb
and one is a noun.

00:16:20.651 --> 00:16:26.050
One way we can represent
them, basically,

00:16:26.051 --> 00:16:28.320
is that there's
an earlier work, I think,

00:16:28.321 --> 00:16:29.700
by Yoav Goldberg about

00:16:29.701 --> 00:16:32.090
structural word embeddings in

00:16:32.091 --> 00:16:34.180
which you take into account
the part of speech.

00:16:34.181 --> 00:16:35.640
So, then, you end up having

00:16:35.641 --> 00:16:38.165
two different representations.

00:16:38.166 --> 00:16:40.230
But the second one, I'm not sure.

00:16:40.231 --> 00:16:44.675
&gt;&gt; I thought you said you
use more than contexts vec-

00:16:44.676 --> 00:16:46.960
&gt;&gt; Right. I compared it to GloVe,

00:16:46.961 --> 00:16:49.165
and in that experiment,

00:16:49.166 --> 00:16:52.370
we haven't observed
meaningful changes.

