00:17:41.161 --> 00:17:42.900
your Bearings:
Lightly-supervised Information

00:17:42.901 --> 00:17:47.100
Extraction with Ladder Networks
avoiding Semantic Drift.

00:17:56.360 --> 00:17:59.110
&gt;&gt; Hi, everybody. I'm Ajay.

00:17:59.111 --> 00:18:00.605
I'm from University of Arizona,

00:18:00.606 --> 00:18:08.520
and this is some work
that we did with Mihai.

00:18:08.521 --> 00:18:12.180
Regarding
semi-supervised learning

00:18:12.181 --> 00:18:14.840
using ladder networks.

00:18:15.940 --> 00:18:21.035
Yeah, so although we
have moved a long way in

00:18:21.036 --> 00:18:22.510
machine learning and we

00:18:22.511 --> 00:18:26.310
have had the performance
has hit the roof,

00:18:26.311 --> 00:18:29.655
there is still the problem
of getting level data.

00:18:29.656 --> 00:18:31.825
As this meme is showing,

00:18:31.826 --> 00:18:34.370
that's the issue, that's
the elephant in the room.

00:18:34.371 --> 00:18:41.060
So, for any kind of
machine learning pipeline,

00:18:41.061 --> 00:18:44.545
the Achilles heel is this,
getting labeled data.

00:18:44.546 --> 00:18:46.930
It's hard to get and it's

00:18:46.931 --> 00:18:50.360
costly and requires a
lot of manual effort.

00:18:50.361 --> 00:18:54.830
Now the whole branches
semi-supervised learning

00:18:54.831 --> 00:18:58.140
is to see if we can
augment to label

00:18:58.141 --> 00:19:00.550
data with lot of
unlabeled observations

00:19:00.551 --> 00:19:01.840
which is available in

00:19:01.841 --> 00:19:06.650
plenty and be able to
generalize from that.

00:19:06.651 --> 00:19:08.895
Well, how do we do that?

00:19:08.896 --> 00:19:12.130
There are lots of techniques
like self-training,

00:19:12.131 --> 00:19:15.895
co-training, level propagation
and you name it.

00:19:15.896 --> 00:19:20.500
Predominantly they all sort
of the state of the art in

00:19:20.501 --> 00:19:22.840
semi-supervised learning
is what is known as

00:19:22.841 --> 00:19:24.670
Bootstrapping which is sort

00:19:24.671 --> 00:19:27.100
of like an iterative algorithm.

00:19:27.101 --> 00:19:29.790
So you have let's suppose

00:19:29.791 --> 00:19:34.975
our task is to learn
names of entities,

00:19:34.976 --> 00:19:37.500
like we want to some kind
of Lexicon induction.

00:19:37.501 --> 00:19:41.200
We start with some small
curated set of seeds.

00:19:41.201 --> 00:19:44.420
For instance we are
learning say persons,

00:19:44.421 --> 00:19:47.010
organizations,
location and so on.

00:19:47.011 --> 00:19:50.430
We start with say
some some cities like

00:19:50.431 --> 00:19:54.610
London or some persons
famous Bill Clinton so on.

00:19:54.611 --> 00:19:58.800
Then we go to the data
and find some patterns

00:19:58.801 --> 00:20:01.095
which occur with these entities

00:20:01.096 --> 00:20:04.690
and say blur is
the capital of something.

00:20:04.691 --> 00:20:07.650
Is a good indicator for

00:20:07.651 --> 00:20:14.530
a location entity
and then we go back,

00:20:14.531 --> 00:20:17.080
we get some new
candidates from this.

00:20:17.081 --> 00:20:18.890
For instance in this case it's

00:20:18.891 --> 00:20:20.615
New Delhi which is
not in the seeds,

00:20:20.616 --> 00:20:22.530
we add it, and

00:20:22.531 --> 00:20:26.005
we keep growing this in
an iterative manner.

00:20:26.006 --> 00:20:29.405
Now one of the
fundamental problems

00:20:29.406 --> 00:20:33.455
of Bootstrapping is
that of semantic drift.

00:20:33.456 --> 00:20:35.025
We'll see with an example here.

00:20:35.026 --> 00:20:37.995
So suppose we are learning
female names like,

00:20:37.996 --> 00:20:40.060
you start with Susan, Rose,

00:20:40.061 --> 00:20:41.910
and so on and then you go to

00:20:41.911 --> 00:20:43.945
your data and you
find some patterns,

00:20:43.946 --> 00:20:45.410
you find some good patterns which

00:20:45.411 --> 00:20:47.070
will indicate women names.

00:20:47.071 --> 00:20:49.930
Or you also find
some patterns which

00:20:49.931 --> 00:20:53.090
might indicate flower names.

00:20:53.091 --> 00:20:55.395
So in this case, if the algorithm

00:20:55.396 --> 00:20:58.060
picks up those patterns,

00:20:58.061 --> 00:21:00.830
then we add some candidate
entities which are

00:21:00.831 --> 00:21:05.160
actually not women names
but flower names.

00:21:05.161 --> 00:21:08.305
So we are drifting from
the original semantics

00:21:08.306 --> 00:21:10.350
of what we intend to

00:21:10.351 --> 00:21:13.355
capture in this. So
this is semantic drift.

00:21:13.356 --> 00:21:20.810
Now, one of the
fundamental observations

00:21:20.811 --> 00:21:23.580
and this is our hypothesis
is that this is caused by

00:21:23.581 --> 00:21:28.175
the iterative nature of
the algorithm of bootstrapping.

00:21:28.176 --> 00:21:31.155
The way to avoid this,

00:21:31.156 --> 00:21:33.600
I mean this is
shown also in a lot

00:21:33.601 --> 00:21:36.820
of related work that
it's unavoidable.

00:21:36.821 --> 00:21:39.740
We are sort of somehow
mitigate this.

00:21:39.741 --> 00:21:42.380
Now what do we do? How
do we mitigate this?

00:21:42.381 --> 00:21:45.250
So there are ways to

00:21:45.251 --> 00:21:47.755
actually mitigate it
in the same framework,

00:21:47.756 --> 00:21:49.670
but how about changing
the framework?

00:21:49.671 --> 00:21:51.210
How about not doing this

00:21:51.211 --> 00:21:54.575
iterative but doing something
in an one-shot manner.

00:21:54.576 --> 00:21:56.965
Now there is a lot
of recent work,

00:21:56.966 --> 00:21:58.640
some recent work
actually which does

00:21:58.641 --> 00:22:00.200
semi-supervised learning not in

00:22:00.201 --> 00:22:02.950
this iterative manner but
in a one-shot manner.

00:22:02.951 --> 00:22:07.855
Much of this is in the
image processing community.

00:22:07.856 --> 00:22:12.920
This we term as one-shot
learning and in this work,

00:22:12.921 --> 00:22:18.045
we focused on one such technique
called Ladder Networks,

00:22:18.046 --> 00:22:21.740
which perform
significantly well on

00:22:21.741 --> 00:22:24.625
a semi-supervised image
classification task

00:22:24.626 --> 00:22:28.525
beating the hell out
of all the baselines.

00:22:28.526 --> 00:22:31.490
Now what's Ladder Networks?

00:22:31.491 --> 00:22:37.255
So it's basically a deep
denoising auto-encoder.

00:22:37.256 --> 00:22:41.045
Now, just like a general
regular auto encoder,

00:22:41.046 --> 00:22:44.310
it has an encoder-decoder
pair and you

00:22:44.311 --> 00:22:47.580
start with some entity
and you try

00:22:47.581 --> 00:22:50.430
and predict back the entity
that's regular auto

00:22:50.431 --> 00:22:54.160
encoder by adding some noise
which is the denoising part.

00:22:54.161 --> 00:22:59.240
The idea is that we
want to like learn

00:22:59.241 --> 00:23:02.280
some abstractions from noise

00:23:02.281 --> 00:23:04.740
so that we learn
some good features.

00:23:04.741 --> 00:23:09.125
Now, what is one of
the key differences

00:23:09.126 --> 00:23:11.590
between a regular
auto encoder and

00:23:11.591 --> 00:23:14.810
denoising auto-encoder is
that of skip connections.

00:23:14.811 --> 00:23:17.150
Now these...

00:23:17.151 --> 00:23:22.650
In a denoising auto-encoder
it just generates back

00:23:22.651 --> 00:23:25.660
the input but with

00:23:25.661 --> 00:23:27.380
these skip connections you also

00:23:27.381 --> 00:23:29.285
generate the intermediate layers.

00:23:29.286 --> 00:23:31.390
Now as a result of this,

00:23:31.391 --> 00:23:36.825
now what happens is it
makes the network modular,

00:23:36.826 --> 00:23:41.500
just like hierarchical
latent variable models.

00:23:41.501 --> 00:23:43.630
That's one of the key
differentiating factors.

00:23:43.631 --> 00:23:45.385
Now, along with that,

00:23:45.386 --> 00:23:48.910
there is another aspect
which is you

00:23:48.911 --> 00:23:52.565
have another clean encoder
which does not have noise.

00:23:52.566 --> 00:23:55.410
Now this is
predominantly used for

00:23:55.411 --> 00:23:59.420
those examples in
your data which have

00:23:59.421 --> 00:24:02.570
labels and you use

00:24:03.050 --> 00:24:05.805
supervised cost to learn

00:24:05.806 --> 00:24:07.975
and backprop through the network.

00:24:07.976 --> 00:24:11.930
You could use any of
your supervised costs here.

00:24:11.931 --> 00:24:13.355
Now the other aspect,

00:24:13.356 --> 00:24:15.305
the other differentiated aspect

00:24:15.306 --> 00:24:17.110
is that the cost function is

00:24:17.111 --> 00:24:21.245
a combination of that
and the region ratio,

00:24:21.246 --> 00:24:23.820
the difference between
the reconstruction

00:24:23.821 --> 00:24:27.810
and the clean target
encoders outputs.

00:24:27.811 --> 00:24:31.965
So that is known as
the reconstruction cost.

00:24:31.966 --> 00:24:34.830
Now these are the two things

00:24:34.831 --> 00:24:38.905
which by doing this it's sort of

00:24:38.906 --> 00:24:43.550
giving us a framework
to actually add

00:24:43.551 --> 00:24:48.975
both labeled and unlabeled data.

00:24:48.976 --> 00:24:51.270
So we look at the task of

00:24:51.271 --> 00:24:53.510
named entity classification
which is sort

00:24:53.511 --> 00:24:56.190
of similar to the induction
that I was talking about,

00:24:56.191 --> 00:24:58.510
which is basically we are given

00:24:58.511 --> 00:25:02.190
some text and the entities
are highlighted.

00:25:02.191 --> 00:25:06.050
We know the demarcations
with entities like here,

00:25:06.051 --> 00:25:11.710
and we want to basically find
the class of the entity.

00:25:11.711 --> 00:25:14.280
We do that by the following.

00:25:14.281 --> 00:25:15.290
So this is how we

00:25:15.291 --> 00:25:17.710
prepare the input to
the Ladder Network.

00:25:17.711 --> 00:25:22.965
So, we have these N-gram
patterns around the entity,

00:25:22.966 --> 00:25:27.545
and we use preferred embeddings
to actually populate

00:25:27.546 --> 00:25:33.205
the tokens of the different
patterns and the words,

00:25:33.206 --> 00:25:35.610
and then we have like

00:25:35.611 --> 00:25:38.460
an averaging layer
which averages and

00:25:38.461 --> 00:25:44.139
finds the representation for
the entity and the context,

00:25:44.140 --> 00:25:47.285
which is a bunch of N-grams

00:25:47.286 --> 00:25:51.020
around the entity and
we concatenate them.

00:25:51.021 --> 00:25:54.125
Okay, I think I have to move.

00:25:54.126 --> 00:25:58.305
So, this is the input
and then this is fed to

00:25:58.306 --> 00:26:00.535
Ladder Network framework which is

00:26:00.536 --> 00:26:03.515
a simple feed forward network,

00:26:03.516 --> 00:26:05.910
it's a two layer feed
forward network.

00:26:05.911 --> 00:26:09.860
The noise that we add here
is like Gaussian noise,

00:26:09.861 --> 00:26:13.720
standard Gaussian noise
and it's just

00:26:13.721 --> 00:26:15.580
like the one that

00:26:15.581 --> 00:26:18.095
is used in Ladder Networks
for image processing.

00:26:18.096 --> 00:26:20.740
Now, coming to our experiments,

00:26:20.741 --> 00:26:22.190
so we use two datasets,

00:26:22.191 --> 00:26:24.960
CoNLL and Ontonotes, and
these are the baselines.

00:26:24.961 --> 00:26:26.620
One of the baselines

00:26:26.621 --> 00:26:30.440
is state of the art
Bootstrapping system,

00:26:30.441 --> 00:26:32.800
the other is the well-known
label Propagation system,

00:26:32.801 --> 00:26:35.130
and we started
some initial seeds which are

00:26:35.131 --> 00:26:39.355
random and so very small sliver
of the training dataset.

00:26:39.356 --> 00:26:42.810
We start as the seeds

00:26:42.811 --> 00:26:44.840
and you have equal representation
in other categories.

00:26:44.841 --> 00:26:47.260
Reason, we use the same seeds

00:26:47.261 --> 00:26:50.570
for like all the
baselines and labels.

00:26:50.571 --> 00:26:52.340
So, these are the results.

00:26:52.341 --> 00:26:55.665
So, what we have here is
the precision versus throughput.

00:26:55.666 --> 00:26:58.020
While here we see

00:26:58.021 --> 00:27:00.745
that at each epoch,
these are the baselines.

00:27:00.746 --> 00:27:05.425
So we add some
entities to the pool

00:27:05.426 --> 00:27:08.485
and this is what
the average precision

00:27:08.486 --> 00:27:11.690
looks like over time.

00:27:11.691 --> 00:27:13.690
This is the Ladder
Network's performance.

00:27:13.691 --> 00:27:16.565
We see a phenomenal improvement,

00:27:16.566 --> 00:27:17.900
close to 62 percent to

00:27:17.901 --> 00:27:20.300
100 percent on
the Ontonotes dataset.

00:27:20.301 --> 00:27:22.120
So there might be a number of

00:27:22.121 --> 00:27:24.230
reasons why this works well.

00:27:24.231 --> 00:27:27.965
So one might be that,

00:27:27.966 --> 00:27:31.505
we are using
multiple deep layers,

00:27:31.506 --> 00:27:32.540
the other is noise,

00:27:32.541 --> 00:27:34.470
which might act as
a regularization.

00:27:34.471 --> 00:27:35.905
But one thing we did notice,

00:27:35.906 --> 00:27:38.170
is because of the flatness
we can say that

00:27:38.171 --> 00:27:40.795
it is sort of mitigating
semantic drift in some sense.

00:27:40.796 --> 00:27:45.700
Now we did some more experiments
where we increase

00:27:45.701 --> 00:27:47.660
the amount of supervision that we

00:27:47.661 --> 00:27:50.360
give and obviously there
are no surprises here,

00:27:50.361 --> 00:27:51.770
we see that the more
the supervision

00:27:51.771 --> 00:27:53.520
the better the result is.

00:27:53.521 --> 00:27:56.370
So these are
some concluding remarks.

00:27:56.371 --> 00:27:59.475
So future directions
is that we want to

00:27:59.476 --> 00:28:02.900
incorporate this to other tasks
like relation extraction,

00:28:02.901 --> 00:28:04.810
where liberated as hard to get by

00:28:04.811 --> 00:28:08.090
and try different
encoders like CNN,

00:28:08.091 --> 00:28:13.365
RNN and so on, and also
focus on interpretability.

00:28:13.366 --> 00:28:15.330
The reason why these had

00:28:15.331 --> 00:28:18.030
these N-grams rather than
just averaging the rules,

00:28:18.031 --> 00:28:19.845
is that we wanted
to actually track

00:28:19.846 --> 00:28:22.140
back which set of
patterns actually

00:28:22.141 --> 00:28:24.680
led to a decision

00:28:24.681 --> 00:28:26.720
which is sort of like
explaining them all.

00:28:26.721 --> 00:28:29.605
So we want to like down
the rules and so on so yeah.

00:28:29.606 --> 00:28:32.780
That's all I have. Thanks.

00:28:35.840 --> 00:28:40.560
&gt;&gt; We have time for
one or two questions.

00:28:47.910 --> 00:28:50.735
&gt;&gt; Have you covered cases where

00:28:50.736 --> 00:28:53.940
semantic drift might not
be such a bad thing?

00:28:53.941 --> 00:28:57.735
Like for example I can
think of scenarios where

00:28:57.736 --> 00:29:04.925
you might be perpetuating
biases of certain kinds.

00:29:04.926 --> 00:29:10.270
If you don't incorporate
semantic drift,

00:29:10.271 --> 00:29:13.065
is this something you
have ideas about,

00:29:13.066 --> 00:29:14.940
like if you see female names for

00:29:14.941 --> 00:29:18.680
example always with
secretaries or whatever,

00:29:19.850 --> 00:29:23.220
that might be a little bit
of a problem, right?

00:29:23.221 --> 00:29:26.780
&gt;&gt; Well, so the the
underlying assumption is

00:29:26.781 --> 00:29:29.890
that the classes that

00:29:29.891 --> 00:29:33.050
you're trying to learn
are mutually exclusive,

00:29:33.530 --> 00:29:37.340
there's very little overlap.

00:29:37.341 --> 00:29:40.200
Yes, we might be
perpetuating biases.

00:29:40.201 --> 00:29:44.435
Sometimes bias may be
good in the sense that,

00:29:44.436 --> 00:29:46.360
like for instance, in this case,

00:29:46.361 --> 00:29:49.780
if the dataset had lot of, say,

00:29:49.781 --> 00:29:51.190
person labels, then you

00:29:51.191 --> 00:29:53.490
would want to predict
more person labels.

00:29:53.491 --> 00:29:56.230
But as the other algorithms
which were iterative,

00:29:56.231 --> 00:29:58.590
we're not capturing
this kind of a bias.

00:29:58.591 --> 00:30:00.590
But bias is bad as well,

00:30:00.591 --> 00:30:03.960
and I don't have an answer
to that question. Yeah.

00:30:03.961 --> 00:30:06.030
&gt;&gt; Unfortunately, that's
all the time we have,

00:30:06.031 --> 00:30:07.350
but let's thank
the speaker again.

00:30:07.351 --> 00:30:16.290
The next presenter is

00:30:16.291 --> 00:30:17.430
Xing Zeng with Infrequent

00:30:17.431 --> 00:30:19.139
Discourse Relation Identification

00:30:19.140 --> 00:30:21.460
Using Data Programming.

00:30:35.330 --> 00:30:38.190
&gt;&gt; Hello, everyone. I'm Xing Zeng

00:30:38.191 --> 00:30:40.350
from University of
British Columbia.

00:30:40.351 --> 00:30:43.830
Today, I'm going to present
our work titled here.

00:30:43.831 --> 00:30:46.485
Discourse parsing
is a important text

00:30:46.486 --> 00:30:47.805
in natural language processing,

00:30:47.806 --> 00:30:51.085
it focus on parsing text into
its rhetorical structure.

00:30:51.086 --> 00:30:53.520
Here, we have example of what

00:30:53.521 --> 00:30:54.960
the rhetorical structure of

00:30:54.961 --> 00:30:56.475
these piece of text looks like.

00:30:56.476 --> 00:30:58.990
Ideally, this should
be a result of

00:30:58.991 --> 00:31:01.600
a discourse parsing software
operated on this piece of

00:31:01.601 --> 00:31:04.650
text because this
rhetorical structure

00:31:04.651 --> 00:31:06.040
includes a lot of information

00:31:06.041 --> 00:31:07.580
how they attack the structure,

00:31:07.581 --> 00:31:09.030
it's extremely useful for

00:31:09.031 --> 00:31:11.290
a wide-range of
downstream NLP tasks,

00:31:11.291 --> 00:31:13.900
like summarization and
sentiment analysis.

00:31:13.901 --> 00:31:15.635
It can be done in two steps,

00:31:15.636 --> 00:31:16.640
although it's not necessary

00:31:16.641 --> 00:31:18.055
required to be done in two steps.

00:31:18.056 --> 00:31:20.165
First, you just create
this tree first,

00:31:20.166 --> 00:31:22.805
and then you assign the
relations on the tree node.

00:31:22.806 --> 00:31:26.075
Here, the relations are
the purpose and the list.

00:31:26.076 --> 00:31:30.005
In our work, we are mostly
focusing on the second task.

00:31:30.006 --> 00:31:32.590
The performance of
the existing methods has been

00:31:32.591 --> 00:31:35.770
overall working very good
for the second task.

00:31:35.771 --> 00:31:37.840
This result from the
state of the art

00:31:37.841 --> 00:31:40.375
Discourse Parser
has a performance

00:31:40.376 --> 00:31:45.610
of 59.7 on the Micro F1
on all 18 relations.

00:31:45.611 --> 00:31:48.660
However, this metrics
hides a lot of detail.

00:31:48.661 --> 00:31:51.300
If we look at the F1
performance for relations,

00:31:51.301 --> 00:31:53.305
the result is less exciting.

00:31:53.306 --> 00:31:57.200
We have some relations that

00:31:57.201 --> 00:31:59.530
has been working
very well over all,

00:31:59.531 --> 00:32:02.280
but we also have those
that has been working

00:32:02.281 --> 00:32:06.125
not as good like evaluation
or topic change.

00:32:06.126 --> 00:32:08.630
We try to evaluate what causes

00:32:08.631 --> 00:32:11.285
these relations to be
not working too well.

00:32:11.286 --> 00:32:13.230
Not surprisingly, we see

00:32:13.231 --> 00:32:21.680
that for those relation that
has less training data,

00:32:21.681 --> 00:32:23.685
it usually perform pretty bad,

00:32:23.686 --> 00:32:25.620
and for those that have usually

00:32:25.621 --> 00:32:28.260
more data, it performs better.

00:32:28.261 --> 00:32:30.585
Given by this observation,

00:32:30.586 --> 00:32:32.520
we propose to use a, I'm sorry.

00:32:32.521 --> 00:32:33.700
&gt;&gt; Do you want to
use this instead?

00:32:33.701 --> 00:32:35.190
&gt;&gt; Yeah, this thing
is more convenient.

00:32:35.191 --> 00:32:43.690
Again, by this-. Given
by this observe-.

00:32:44.930 --> 00:32:48.000
Okay. Given by this observation,

00:32:48.001 --> 00:32:51.020
we propose to use
a semi-supervised method to add

00:32:51.021 --> 00:32:52.090
more training data for

00:32:52.091 --> 00:32:55.600
infrequent relations to
boost their performance.

00:32:55.601 --> 00:32:58.110
In order to do so, we
will like to apply

00:32:58.111 --> 00:33:00.015
a framework called
data programming

00:33:00.016 --> 00:33:01.620
introduced by a group of

00:33:01.621 --> 00:33:04.565
researchers from
Stanford in 2016.

00:33:04.566 --> 00:33:06.500
In this framework, one could

00:33:06.501 --> 00:33:08.330
use domain knowledge to create

00:33:08.331 --> 00:33:12.475
a heuristic functions to label
part of the training data.

00:33:12.476 --> 00:33:15.460
Then, these heuristic function
can be combined

00:33:15.461 --> 00:33:18.645
together much like how in
sampling method will work.

00:33:18.646 --> 00:33:21.880
The way how they combine
them together is by training

00:33:21.881 --> 00:33:25.900
a graphical model like the
one shown to the right.

00:33:27.040 --> 00:33:30.470
This graphical model could
learn the accuracy of each of

00:33:30.471 --> 00:33:33.180
heuristics by comparing
them against each other.

00:33:33.181 --> 00:33:35.675
In situations where a heuristic

00:33:35.676 --> 00:33:38.935
might possibly foul
to infer a label,

00:33:38.936 --> 00:33:40.550
it can be just treated as

00:33:40.551 --> 00:33:42.810
a hidden value in
the graphical model.

00:33:42.811 --> 00:33:45.885
The true label which
is unobserved is also

00:33:45.886 --> 00:33:49.630
treated as a hidden value
in the graphical model.

00:33:49.631 --> 00:33:53.120
Once the graphical model
is trained for

00:33:53.121 --> 00:33:55.440
every unlabeled instances given

00:33:55.441 --> 00:33:58.070
by their output from
all the heuristics function,

00:33:58.071 --> 00:34:01.100
one could infer
a probabilistic distribution

00:34:01.101 --> 00:34:03.465
over all of its possible labels.

00:34:03.466 --> 00:34:05.750
However, this type of framework

00:34:05.751 --> 00:34:08.100
cannot be directly
applied to our problem

00:34:08.101 --> 00:34:10.780
because it's virtually impossible

00:34:10.781 --> 00:34:13.335
to create heuristic function
for relations.

00:34:13.336 --> 00:34:15.550
So we try to use

00:34:15.551 --> 00:34:17.075
machine learning method to

00:34:17.076 --> 00:34:19.510
replace a heuristic
function here.

00:34:19.511 --> 00:34:21.560
This is how we create

00:34:21.561 --> 00:34:24.700
the heuristic function
or labeling functions,

00:34:24.701 --> 00:34:26.470
we randomly sample half of

00:34:26.471 --> 00:34:28.765
the labelled data for four times.

00:34:28.766 --> 00:34:31.715
This give a four
different datasets

00:34:31.716 --> 00:34:33.080
for different both still or

00:34:33.081 --> 00:34:34.720
might be overlapping datasets,

00:34:34.721 --> 00:34:36.900
and then on each
of the datasets we

00:34:36.901 --> 00:34:40.245
create one neural network
classifier on top of it.

00:34:40.246 --> 00:34:45.640
This is similar to
the method of bagging.

00:34:45.641 --> 00:34:48.090
And then, for each of
these classifiers,

00:34:48.091 --> 00:34:50.980
we pass the unlabeled
data into it and

00:34:50.981 --> 00:34:54.585
let it output its label as
well as confidence score.

00:34:54.586 --> 00:34:56.850
The confidence score which is

00:34:56.851 --> 00:34:58.720
the softmax layer coming from

00:34:58.721 --> 00:35:00.930
the last layer of the
neural network will be

00:35:00.931 --> 00:35:04.870
used to filter out

00:35:04.871 --> 00:35:08.095
those that have a relatively
lower confidence score.

00:35:08.096 --> 00:35:11.655
We try with two variants
of filtering,

00:35:11.656 --> 00:35:15.260
one is to just set up a uniform
boundary for all labels,

00:35:15.261 --> 00:35:17.040
as long as the
confidence score is

00:35:17.041 --> 00:35:18.530
greater than
pre-defined boundary,

00:35:18.531 --> 00:35:22.010
it will be kept, otherwise
we'll discard it.

00:35:22.011 --> 00:35:24.390
We also try another variant which

00:35:24.391 --> 00:35:26.775
we call as dynamic boundary.

00:35:26.776 --> 00:35:28.880
For each label, the boundary is

00:35:28.881 --> 00:35:31.410
dynamically selected so
that the distribution of

00:35:31.411 --> 00:35:34.870
a label being selected
is the same as

00:35:34.871 --> 00:35:36.990
the distribution of the label

00:35:36.991 --> 00:35:39.125
already in the training dataset.

00:35:39.126 --> 00:35:41.255
These two ways will

00:35:41.256 --> 00:35:44.870
generate different results
as we will later see.

00:35:45.050 --> 00:35:48.995
After we filter out
the labels, the model,

00:35:48.996 --> 00:35:50.425
we can directly invoke

00:35:50.426 --> 00:35:51.870
the data programming framework

00:35:51.871 --> 00:35:54.065
and train a graphical
model like this.

00:35:54.066 --> 00:35:56.995
Again, here, white
denodes hidden value

00:35:56.996 --> 00:35:59.870
and dark denodes value
that's observed.

00:36:00.500 --> 00:36:02.895
After we train this,

00:36:02.896 --> 00:36:05.760
we can train our final model
where the final model will

00:36:05.761 --> 00:36:07.895
utilize both the
supervised dataset

00:36:07.896 --> 00:36:10.070
and the unsupervised dataset,

00:36:10.071 --> 00:36:11.630
and you'll have
a loss function of

00:36:11.631 --> 00:36:13.270
this form which takes into

00:36:13.271 --> 00:36:17.150
both the supervised part
and the unsupervised part.

00:36:17.151 --> 00:36:19.270
The supervised part
and unsupervised part

00:36:19.271 --> 00:36:21.790
will both use a cross
entropy loss function.

00:36:21.791 --> 00:36:23.540
The supervised part will use

00:36:23.541 --> 00:36:25.860
the true label as its supervision
in the cross entropy,

00:36:25.861 --> 00:36:28.180
where the unsupervised
part will use

00:36:28.181 --> 00:36:30.430
the label distribution coming

00:36:30.431 --> 00:36:32.940
from data programming
as a supervision.

00:36:32.941 --> 00:36:37.290
For the experiments,
we do our experiments

00:36:37.291 --> 00:36:39.670
on the RST Discourse
Treebank dataset

00:36:39.671 --> 00:36:41.755
also called the RST-DT.

00:36:41.756 --> 00:36:43.300
For the unlabeled dataset,

00:36:43.301 --> 00:36:46.260
we use the New York Time
Annotated Corpus which

00:36:46.261 --> 00:36:49.290
are also a new Stockman
like the RST-DT is.

00:36:49.291 --> 00:36:51.470
In the RST-DT, there are

00:36:51.471 --> 00:36:54.255
these top ten most
frequent relations.

00:36:54.256 --> 00:36:57.225
These top ten relations forms

00:36:57.226 --> 00:36:58.610
almost 90 percent of

00:36:58.611 --> 00:37:02.095
the total training data
in the RST-DT dataset,

00:37:02.096 --> 00:37:04.935
and the rest of
the eight relations

00:37:04.936 --> 00:37:08.635
only take up 10 percent
of the dataset.

00:37:08.636 --> 00:37:10.455
In our work, we are mainly

00:37:10.456 --> 00:37:12.700
focused on improving
the performance of

00:37:12.701 --> 00:37:14.450
the lower eight without

00:37:14.451 --> 00:37:17.560
having a negative effect
on the top 10.

00:37:18.300 --> 00:37:21.235
Some extra experimental settings,

00:37:21.236 --> 00:37:22.790
data programming which has

00:37:22.791 --> 00:37:25.190
a graphical model into
it is trained using

00:37:25.191 --> 00:37:27.220
stochastic gradient descent on

00:37:27.221 --> 00:37:30.020
the maximum likelihood
estimation of the observation.

00:37:30.021 --> 00:37:32.800
For the classifier, we

00:37:32.801 --> 00:37:36.175
use as labeling functions
and also final method,

00:37:36.176 --> 00:37:38.250
for now we are always using

00:37:38.251 --> 00:37:41.335
a simple one hidden layer
feed-forward neural network,

00:37:41.336 --> 00:37:43.770
we try two variants of it.

00:37:43.771 --> 00:37:45.850
One is a standard one that does

00:37:45.851 --> 00:37:48.140
not have dropouts
and parameter to

00:37:48.141 --> 00:37:49.150
be loosely tuned in

00:37:49.151 --> 00:37:50.690
cross validation to make

00:37:50.691 --> 00:37:52.980
sure that the loss
no longer changes.

00:37:52.981 --> 00:37:55.340
We also try another one
which has dropout

00:37:55.341 --> 00:37:57.620
enabled and parameter to be more

00:37:57.621 --> 00:37:59.580
carefully tuned in
cross validation in order

00:37:59.581 --> 00:38:02.635
to reach the highest
micro-average F1.

00:38:02.636 --> 00:38:05.130
For the features we use
with the classifier,

00:38:05.131 --> 00:38:08.375
we use the concatenation of
the both of the feature.

00:38:08.376 --> 00:38:11.190
First is the human
engineered feature

00:38:11.191 --> 00:38:12.835
from the previous
state of the art,

00:38:12.836 --> 00:38:14.360
and the next one is

00:38:14.361 --> 00:38:15.880
the Word2Vec embeddings of

00:38:15.881 --> 00:38:19.000
the boundary words
on a discourse unit.

00:38:19.790 --> 00:38:22.175
Here are the results we get,

00:38:22.176 --> 00:38:24.525
we evaluate it on four metrics.

00:38:24.526 --> 00:38:26.810
The first metric is the micro F,

00:38:26.811 --> 00:38:28.600
all of them are F1 score,

00:38:28.601 --> 00:38:31.250
the first metric is
the micro F1 score

00:38:31.251 --> 00:38:33.125
which is the most common metrics,

00:38:33.126 --> 00:38:36.520
and the rest of the- are
macro F1 score which

00:38:36.521 --> 00:38:40.165
basically is the F1 score
on each of the relations,

00:38:40.166 --> 00:38:42.190
but just taking
the average without

00:38:42.191 --> 00:38:45.410
taking into the account
of their sizes.

00:38:45.411 --> 00:38:48.640
Let's look at
the first two column

00:38:48.641 --> 00:38:50.040
which shows the performance

00:38:50.041 --> 00:38:51.940
of the uniform boundary
for neural network

00:38:51.941 --> 00:38:54.095
one with or without
data programming.

00:38:54.096 --> 00:38:55.795
We can see that for this one,

00:38:55.796 --> 00:38:57.740
the one that has
the data programming

00:38:57.741 --> 00:38:59.350
significantly output one that

00:38:59.351 --> 00:39:01.550
does not have data programming.

00:39:01.551 --> 00:39:04.250
This is not the case
for a neural network

00:39:04.251 --> 00:39:07.320
two where we actually have
a decrease in performance.

00:39:07.321 --> 00:39:09.990
However, the result changes if

00:39:09.991 --> 00:39:13.105
we change our way to
do the filterings.

00:39:13.106 --> 00:39:14.770
If we use dynamic boundary

00:39:14.771 --> 00:39:17.030
instead for neural network one,

00:39:17.031 --> 00:39:19.420
we still have improvement
in performance

00:39:19.421 --> 00:39:22.545
although it's slightly less
than what it used to be,

00:39:22.546 --> 00:39:25.650
and we are able to
improve three out

00:39:25.651 --> 00:39:28.785
of the four scores for NN2.

00:39:28.786 --> 00:39:31.090
We are unable to
improve the score on

00:39:31.091 --> 00:39:34.030
their micro average F1, however.

00:39:34.490 --> 00:39:37.375
In order to check in
detail that our method

00:39:37.376 --> 00:39:39.689
actually help
the infrequent relations,

00:39:39.690 --> 00:39:41.180
we check the performance

00:39:41.181 --> 00:39:44.200
of all 18 relations individually.

00:39:44.201 --> 00:39:46.480
Here, I'm showing
the performance of

00:39:46.481 --> 00:39:49.280
the 10 most frequent
relations order by

00:39:49.281 --> 00:39:51.580
their frequency in
the training dataset denote

00:39:51.581 --> 00:39:54.480
by the second column,

00:39:54.481 --> 00:39:56.350
and the third column

00:39:56.351 --> 00:39:58.480
is the performance without
using data programming,

00:39:58.481 --> 00:40:00.020
and the fourth column

00:40:00.021 --> 00:40:02.100
is the one that has
data programming.

00:40:02.101 --> 00:40:03.825
You can see that here,

00:40:03.826 --> 00:40:06.130
we reach our original
goals such that

00:40:06.131 --> 00:40:11.650
we usually do not

00:40:11.651 --> 00:40:14.060
have a significant decrease
in performance,

00:40:14.061 --> 00:40:16.475
and we actually have
some improvements

00:40:16.476 --> 00:40:18.975
which is quite unexpected.

00:40:18.976 --> 00:40:21.610
For infrequent relations, we

00:40:21.611 --> 00:40:23.610
can reach some huge improvement

00:40:23.611 --> 00:40:25.380
on those that has been
originally working

00:40:25.381 --> 00:40:27.905
extremely bad except
for a topic comment,

00:40:27.906 --> 00:40:29.670
and we mostly does not have

00:40:29.671 --> 00:40:31.725
a negative result
except for summary.

00:40:31.726 --> 00:40:35.170
We're still investigating
why is summary so different.

00:40:35.490 --> 00:40:38.320
As you can see, our method does

00:40:38.321 --> 00:40:41.120
help improve the performance
of the infrequent relations.

00:40:41.121 --> 00:40:42.860
However, our
performance currently

00:40:42.861 --> 00:40:44.885
still did not beat
this state-of-the-art.

00:40:44.886 --> 00:40:47.630
So, we propose
some future directions.

00:40:47.631 --> 00:40:49.090
One future direction is to

00:40:49.091 --> 00:40:51.415
utilize other strategies
like meta learning.

00:40:51.416 --> 00:40:54.535
One is to develop
other future classifier,

00:40:54.536 --> 00:40:55.920
and another one is to use

00:40:55.921 --> 00:40:57.565
a deep learning based
method instead,

00:40:57.566 --> 00:41:05.580
and that's all.

00:41:05.581 --> 00:41:07.275
&gt;&gt; Is Shobhit here?
We need Shobhit.

00:41:07.276 --> 00:41:09.935
&gt;&gt; Yeah, Shobhit didn't come
up [inaudible] and then,

00:41:09.936 --> 00:41:12.420
we have time for
one good question.

00:41:20.690 --> 00:41:24.310
&gt;&gt; All right. Well, let's
thank our speaker again.

00:41:33.260 --> 00:41:36.550
Next up, we have
Shobhit Hathi presenting

00:41:36.551 --> 00:41:39.160
Community Member Retrieval
on Social Media Using

00:41:39.161 --> 00:41:48.400
Textual Information. You
can use that if you like.

00:41:48.401 --> 00:41:51.105
&gt;&gt; Hi everyone,
my name is Shobhit,

00:41:51.106 --> 00:41:52.830
and I'm presenting our paper on

00:41:52.831 --> 00:41:54.070
Community Member Retrieval on

00:41:54.071 --> 00:41:56.080
Social Media Using
Textual Information.

00:41:56.081 --> 00:42:01.550
So, just to introduce
it with a scenario.

00:42:01.551 --> 00:42:04.665
Say, a user selects
a handful of Twitter users,

00:42:04.666 --> 00:42:06.200
and wants to find more people

00:42:06.201 --> 00:42:08.010
that belong in this community.

00:42:08.011 --> 00:42:09.640
So, in this example,

00:42:09.641 --> 00:42:12.425
the user selected a handful
of NLP researchers

00:42:12.426 --> 00:42:14.090
and would want to
find more people

00:42:14.091 --> 00:42:15.830
that tweet like these users.

00:42:15.831 --> 00:42:18.300
The detection model
that we propose will

00:42:18.301 --> 00:42:21.270
only use textual features
from the user tweets.

00:42:21.271 --> 00:42:23.320
While most prior work uses

00:42:23.321 --> 00:42:25.595
social network connections
for this type of task,

00:42:25.596 --> 00:42:27.820
the social graph is
not always accessible,

00:42:27.821 --> 00:42:29.705
and not everyone
is well connected,

00:42:29.706 --> 00:42:31.670
so that's why we're only
using textual features.

00:42:31.671 --> 00:42:36.830
Major challenge with modeling

00:42:36.831 --> 00:42:38.130
for this task is that

00:42:38.131 --> 00:42:40.080
the only labeled training data

00:42:40.081 --> 00:42:41.485
comes from the query itself.

00:42:41.486 --> 00:42:43.530
So, the solution we
propose is learning

00:42:43.531 --> 00:42:45.670
similarities and
differences between

00:42:45.671 --> 00:42:48.040
individual people as
a proxy task and hoping that

00:42:48.041 --> 00:42:51.255
this information carries
over to the main task.

00:42:51.256 --> 00:42:52.850
We do this using a person via

00:42:52.851 --> 00:42:55.090
the identification task and

00:42:55.091 --> 00:42:57.780
a large unlabeled
collection of tweets.

00:42:57.781 --> 00:43:01.360
Once we have the features
from this proxy tasks,

00:43:01.361 --> 00:43:04.410
we chain a logistic regression
classifier on top of

00:43:04.411 --> 00:43:07.090
these features whenever
we're asked to

00:43:07.091 --> 00:43:09.680
search for members
of the community.

00:43:09.681 --> 00:43:13.340
So, the main contribution
was the proxy tasks,

00:43:13.341 --> 00:43:15.100
so let's just examine
it real quick.

00:43:15.101 --> 00:43:17.620
We use bag-of-words as

00:43:17.621 --> 00:43:20.725
features across
all the tweets of the users.

00:43:20.726 --> 00:43:22.960
We take two samples of

00:43:22.961 --> 00:43:25.480
a person p's tweets which
in this case would be,

00:43:25.481 --> 00:43:27.640
Yoav Goldberg, and
one sample from

00:43:27.641 --> 00:43:30.780
a person q which in
this case is Elon Musk.

00:43:30.781 --> 00:43:34.145
We define a user embedding
u as the average of

00:43:34.146 --> 00:43:35.610
word embeddings weighted by

00:43:35.611 --> 00:43:38.150
the life frequency
that they appear,

00:43:38.151 --> 00:43:39.995
and we learn in
embedding vector for

00:43:39.996 --> 00:43:42.300
each token of
the vocabulary to minimize

00:43:42.301 --> 00:43:44.490
a margin plus
the distance between

00:43:44.491 --> 00:43:46.745
the two samples of
person p's tweets,

00:43:46.746 --> 00:43:48.180
in this case, Yoav,

00:43:48.181 --> 00:43:50.350
minus the distance
between the sample of

00:43:50.351 --> 00:43:52.400
person p's tweets and
person q's tweets.

00:43:52.401 --> 00:43:55.280
So, intuitively, what we're
learning is what does

00:43:55.281 --> 00:43:56.640
Yoav have in common with

00:43:56.641 --> 00:43:59.270
himself that separates
him from Elon?

00:43:59.271 --> 00:44:04.460
Again, we hope that learning
this representation encodes

00:44:04.461 --> 00:44:06.360
information that
will be useful for

00:44:06.361 --> 00:44:09.345
picking out community
members later on.

00:44:09.346 --> 00:44:12.580
So, the data that we collected,

00:44:12.581 --> 00:44:15.070
we collected 80,000 users

00:44:15.071 --> 00:44:16.800
from Twitter just by
randomly selecting

00:44:16.801 --> 00:44:18.645
a 1,000 trending Twitter topics

00:44:18.646 --> 00:44:21.150
in the US over
some months last spring,

00:44:21.151 --> 00:44:23.470
and we found users tweeting
about these topics

00:44:23.471 --> 00:44:26.775
and collected their 2,000
most recent tweets.

00:44:26.776 --> 00:44:30.550
We also hand created
16 communities which were

00:44:30.551 --> 00:44:32.400
just groups of users
that fit things of

00:44:32.401 --> 00:44:34.730
interest to us and
our friends and colleagues.

00:44:34.731 --> 00:44:36.010
So, the communities that we

00:44:36.011 --> 00:44:37.490
selected are displayed
on the table

00:44:37.491 --> 00:44:41.815
here along with the size
of the communities.

00:44:41.816 --> 00:44:46.260
The vocabulary had 174,000
unique types including

00:44:46.261 --> 00:44:48.310
49,000 bigrams that were selected

00:44:48.311 --> 00:44:51.495
using Pointwise
Mutual Information,

00:44:51.496 --> 00:44:55.445
36,000 usernames and
17,000 hashtags.

00:44:55.446 --> 00:44:58.690
So, the experiment
was configured.

00:44:58.691 --> 00:45:02.030
So, we took the 80,000
general population members

00:45:02.031 --> 00:45:04.110
and split them into 36,000

00:45:04.111 --> 00:45:06.270
to learn the embeddings
for the proxy task,

00:45:06.271 --> 00:45:09.215
1,000 as negative samples
to train the classifier,

00:45:09.216 --> 00:45:12.360
and 43,000 to evaluate the model.

00:45:12.361 --> 00:45:13.860
The way we performed

00:45:13.861 --> 00:45:15.620
the model evaluation was

00:45:15.621 --> 00:45:17.940
by holding a
community member out,

00:45:17.941 --> 00:45:20.305
chaining the logistic
regression classifier

00:45:20.306 --> 00:45:22.380
between the remaining
community members,

00:45:22.381 --> 00:45:23.905
and the thousand
negative samples,

00:45:23.906 --> 00:45:26.605
and performing re-ranking task
where we scored

00:45:26.606 --> 00:45:29.190
the one hold-out community
member against all

00:45:29.191 --> 00:45:31.830
the 43,000 negative samples.

00:45:31.831 --> 00:45:33.380
We performed this for

00:45:33.381 --> 00:45:34.420
every community member for

00:45:34.421 --> 00:45:36.685
every community and
average the results.

00:45:36.686 --> 00:45:38.380
The metrics we use
were area under

00:45:38.381 --> 00:45:41.690
the curve and inverse
mean reciprocal rank.

00:45:41.691 --> 00:45:43.990
So, we tested our RE-ID strategy

00:45:43.991 --> 00:45:46.040
against Word2Vec and Latent

00:45:46.041 --> 00:45:48.300
Dirichlet Allocation
baselines which

00:45:48.301 --> 00:45:50.970
are commonly used for
this type of task.

00:45:50.971 --> 00:45:53.630
What we found is that
our RE-ID features

00:45:53.631 --> 00:45:55.890
do much better than
the baselines.

00:45:55.891 --> 00:45:59.585
You can see that the average
rank really isn't close.

00:45:59.586 --> 00:46:01.080
We found that the RE-ID works

00:46:01.081 --> 00:46:03.445
well even with small queries,

00:46:03.446 --> 00:46:05.560
as you saw in the previous table.

00:46:05.561 --> 00:46:07.920
Some of them had as
few as 11 members.

00:46:07.921 --> 00:46:09.825
We found that when we took

00:46:09.826 --> 00:46:11.670
the pre-trained
Word2Vec embeddings,

00:46:11.671 --> 00:46:14.120
and then trained them
with the RE-ID tests.

00:46:14.121 --> 00:46:15.290
We found that the resulting

00:46:15.291 --> 00:46:16.885
embeddings performed the best.

00:46:16.886 --> 00:46:19.200
So, to examine exactly what

00:46:19.201 --> 00:46:20.720
the proxy tasks we're learning.

00:46:20.721 --> 00:46:22.420
We looked at the words
that changed the

00:46:22.421 --> 00:46:24.480
most between the
Word2Vec embeddings,

00:46:24.481 --> 00:46:26.770
and the Word2Vec embeddings

00:46:26.771 --> 00:46:28.820
later chain using the RE-ID task.

00:46:28.821 --> 00:46:32.005
What we found after clustering
these words is that

00:46:32.006 --> 00:46:34.540
mentioning one of
these words that

00:46:34.541 --> 00:46:35.610
change a lot tells you a lot

00:46:35.611 --> 00:46:37.000
about your likes and dislikes.

00:46:37.001 --> 00:46:39.130
Was this useful in separating

00:46:39.131 --> 00:46:42.060
you from some other
random negative sample?

00:46:42.061 --> 00:46:44.620
So, yeah.

00:46:44.621 --> 00:46:47.840
What we also wanted
to do is find out

00:46:47.841 --> 00:46:50.980
what was considered prototypical
tweet by our classifier.

00:46:50.981 --> 00:46:53.830
So, what words that you can use

00:46:53.831 --> 00:46:55.160
to give the signal that

00:46:55.161 --> 00:46:56.875
you belong to
a certain community.

00:46:56.876 --> 00:46:59.750
We found that the classifier
and the embeddings are

00:46:59.751 --> 00:47:02.880
learning things that seem
intuitively obvious.

00:47:02.881 --> 00:47:04.720
For example, if you talk about

00:47:04.721 --> 00:47:06.200
morphological priors for

00:47:06.201 --> 00:47:08.460
probabilistic neural
word embeddings,

00:47:08.461 --> 00:47:10.365
you're probably an
NLP researcher.

00:47:10.366 --> 00:47:11.855
Whereas, if you talk about

00:47:11.856 --> 00:47:13.850
airline economics interfluidity,

00:47:13.851 --> 00:47:16.170
you're probably an economist.

00:47:16.590 --> 00:47:20.170
So, in conclusion, we
propose a method for

00:47:20.171 --> 00:47:21.410
finding people that tweet

00:47:21.411 --> 00:47:23.770
similarly to a
specified set of users.

00:47:23.771 --> 00:47:25.170
We introduce a person

00:47:25.171 --> 00:47:27.890
re-identification proxy
task that allows us to

00:47:27.891 --> 00:47:29.960
create embeddings
that perform well at

00:47:29.961 --> 00:47:32.700
this task compared to Word2Vec,

00:47:32.701 --> 00:47:34.075
and all the embeddings

00:47:34.076 --> 00:47:37.215
even when the query set
has very few users.

00:47:37.216 --> 00:47:39.520
These embeddings
depend only on texts,

00:47:39.521 --> 00:47:41.460
but are complementary to
social media features,

00:47:41.461 --> 00:47:43.880
so they can be
incorporated as well.

00:47:43.881 --> 00:47:47.495
While we use a bag-of-words
model for simplicity,

00:47:47.496 --> 00:47:50.770
this re-identification objective
can be extended to

00:47:50.771 --> 00:47:55.180
other more structured methods
as well. Thank you.

00:47:59.320 --> 00:48:02.320
&gt;&gt; Any questions?

00:48:02.321 --> 00:48:03.320
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:48:03.321 --> 00:48:07.755
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:48:07.756 --> 00:48:09.685
So, I actually had a quick one.

00:48:09.686 --> 00:48:11.650
The re-identification task is

00:48:11.651 --> 00:48:13.830
from computer vision,
is that correct?

00:48:13.831 --> 00:48:16.970
&gt;&gt; Yes. The objective,

00:48:16.971 --> 00:48:19.330
the triplet loss objective
that we used in

00:48:19.331 --> 00:48:23.570
the VID task is adapted from
a computer vision task.

00:48:23.571 --> 00:48:26.850
&gt;&gt; And that's for

00:48:26.851 --> 00:48:30.730
a closed circuit to make sure

00:48:30.731 --> 00:48:34.095
that the same user that
is appearing again with,

00:48:34.096 --> 00:48:35.600
maybe, a partially occluded face

00:48:35.601 --> 00:48:37.230
is the same as previously?

00:48:37.231 --> 00:48:37.780
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:48:37.781 --> 00:48:39.070
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:48:39.071 --> 00:48:41.600
&gt;&gt; Joel, are you here?
Could you come forward?

00:48:41.601 --> 00:48:44.420
Go ahead.

00:48:44.421 --> 00:48:47.410
&gt;&gt; Any other questions?

00:48:49.740 --> 00:48:52.980
&gt;&gt; Did you use
the Twitter network

00:48:52.981 --> 00:48:55.005
to actually identify people,

00:48:55.006 --> 00:48:59.025
like who are they connected
to and who they retweet?

00:48:59.026 --> 00:49:00.310
&gt;&gt; No.

00:49:00.311 --> 00:49:02.090
We actually explicitly avoided

00:49:02.091 --> 00:49:04.015
using social media features

00:49:04.016 --> 00:49:06.180
because the graph isn't always

00:49:06.181 --> 00:49:09.895
accessible for all users
and for all social media,

00:49:09.896 --> 00:49:12.555
and some people might
not be well connected.

00:49:12.556 --> 00:49:15.070
So if we want to find
people that belong

00:49:15.071 --> 00:49:17.895
in a user interest
group or community,

00:49:17.896 --> 00:49:19.070
we don't want to just find

00:49:19.071 --> 00:49:20.650
the people that are
well-connected to

00:49:20.651 --> 00:49:22.750
the credit set but
also other people

00:49:22.751 --> 00:49:25.150
that have similar interests.

00:49:27.350 --> 00:49:30.770
&gt;&gt; Hi.

00:49:30.771 --> 00:49:32.180
So did you come across

00:49:32.181 --> 00:49:34.895
users who fall under
multiple groups,

00:49:34.896 --> 00:49:36.495
say, for a person,

00:49:36.496 --> 00:49:38.130
Works 95 and tweets about

00:49:38.131 --> 00:49:40.640
NLP stuff and then
when goes back home,

00:49:40.641 --> 00:49:42.310
he tweets about
what he is cooking.

00:49:42.311 --> 00:49:45.490
So there would be
a diverse set of tweets,

00:49:45.491 --> 00:49:47.340
what a user can tweet.

00:49:47.341 --> 00:49:49.430
So did you come across
those instances,

00:49:49.431 --> 00:49:50.535
and how did you handle them?

00:49:50.536 --> 00:49:55.600
&gt;&gt; We did. So the way

00:49:55.601 --> 00:49:56.900
we formulated the problem is

00:49:56.901 --> 00:50:00.155
that you can specify
any query set.

00:50:00.156 --> 00:50:02.770
So it's up to the user to

00:50:02.771 --> 00:50:03.900
specify a query set that

00:50:03.901 --> 00:50:05.555
tweets about things
they're interested in,

00:50:05.556 --> 00:50:09.590
and our model tries

00:50:09.710 --> 00:50:12.450
to find similarities between

00:50:12.451 --> 00:50:13.770
what the community
is tweeting about.

00:50:13.771 --> 00:50:15.640
So if a person tweets about,

00:50:15.641 --> 00:50:18.315
if you go to
the prototypical tweet,

00:50:18.316 --> 00:50:20.240
we had NLP researchers that tweet

00:50:20.241 --> 00:50:22.400
about NLP and then also tweet
about other things,

00:50:22.401 --> 00:50:24.170
where the classifier learns
that what's considered

00:50:24.171 --> 00:50:26.310
a prototypical tweet for
the community would be

00:50:26.311 --> 00:50:28.275
one that uses lots
of words that are

00:50:28.276 --> 00:50:31.720
shared amongst
the community members.

00:50:31.870 --> 00:50:34.420
&gt;&gt; Yeah, we're going to go ahead

00:50:34.421 --> 00:50:36.725
and take some advantage
to do catch up again.

00:50:36.726 --> 00:50:38.950
So let's thank our speaker again.

00:50:43.870 --> 00:50:48.250
And our final speaker for
the morning is Joel Adams,

00:50:48.251 --> 00:50:50.225
doing Semantic similarity on

00:50:50.226 --> 00:50:52.150
conversational speech
between children

00:50:52.151 --> 00:50:54.240
with and without ASD.

00:51:07.290 --> 00:51:12.180
&gt;&gt; All right. Cool. So
hello, my name's Joel Adams.

00:51:12.181 --> 00:51:13.545
I'm from CSLU and

00:51:13.546 --> 00:51:15.050
I'm talking about
semantic similarity and

00:51:15.051 --> 00:51:16.690
differences in
the speech of children

00:51:16.691 --> 00:51:18.525
with and without autism.

00:51:18.526 --> 00:51:22.650
Now, Autism Spectrum Disorder is

00:51:22.651 --> 00:51:24.580
a range of conditions which is

00:51:24.581 --> 00:51:27.340
classified as a
neurodevelopmental disorder.

00:51:27.341 --> 00:51:28.840
It's often associated with

00:51:28.841 --> 00:51:35.615
challenges with
social communication,

00:51:35.616 --> 00:51:38.345
with early case
studies discussing

00:51:38.346 --> 00:51:41.735
intense but specific interests
or repetitive language.

00:51:41.736 --> 00:51:46.775
But we find that
the speech of people with

00:51:46.776 --> 00:51:50.250
ASD diagnosis is at least
as heterogeneous as

00:51:50.251 --> 00:51:53.330
the speech of typically
developing children and people.

00:51:53.331 --> 00:51:55.490
And indeed, Dr.
Stephen Shore who is

00:51:55.491 --> 00:51:57.800
a clinician at Delphi says that,

00:51:57.801 --> 00:51:59.050
"If you know one person
with autism,

00:51:59.051 --> 00:52:00.535
you know one person with autism."

00:52:00.536 --> 00:52:02.780
And in our study,
it seems to be that

00:52:02.781 --> 00:52:05.320
if you've spoken with
one person with autism,

00:52:05.321 --> 00:52:08.125
you've spoken with
one person with autism.

00:52:08.126 --> 00:52:14.900
The study of our lab is
specifically on quantifying

00:52:14.901 --> 00:52:17.520
the variability of speech

00:52:17.521 --> 00:52:21.040
in children with
and without autism.

00:52:21.041 --> 00:52:23.450
So how can we quantify
this in a language domain,

00:52:23.451 --> 00:52:24.520
and specifically for this talk,

00:52:24.521 --> 00:52:25.930
how can we talk about what's

00:52:25.931 --> 00:52:27.650
different in what children speak

00:52:27.651 --> 00:52:30.940
about, with and without autism.

00:52:30.941 --> 00:52:33.845
So let's take a look at
what that might look like.

00:52:33.846 --> 00:52:35.660
What are children talking about?

00:52:35.661 --> 00:52:39.450
Let's imagine that we have
some really short transcripts,

00:52:39.451 --> 00:52:42.455
exaggerate some really short
transcripts of children.

00:52:42.456 --> 00:52:44.890
And this [inaudible]
we have four of them.

00:52:44.891 --> 00:52:46.750
This first one and the
second one, we have,

00:52:46.751 --> 00:52:48.000
"I'm looking for my pet dinosaur

00:52:48.001 --> 00:52:49.005
who just got out of jail,

00:52:49.006 --> 00:52:50.050
and I just made the universe's

00:52:50.051 --> 00:52:52.090
most hottest hot lava hot sauce."

00:52:52.091 --> 00:52:55.615
These seemed clearly
about different subjects,

00:52:55.616 --> 00:52:57.320
and we would expect

00:52:57.321 --> 00:52:59.120
a reasonable semantic model
be able to show

00:52:59.121 --> 00:53:01.205
that those are different.

00:53:01.206 --> 00:53:03.240
But let's imagine we talk to

00:53:03.241 --> 00:53:05.525
two more children and
the third one talks about,

00:53:05.526 --> 00:53:07.150
"I tucked my dinosaur into bed",

00:53:07.151 --> 00:53:08.990
the fourth says, "That's
a lambeosaurus."

00:53:08.991 --> 00:53:12.220
So the third seems to be
similar to the first,

00:53:12.221 --> 00:53:14.090
it's about probably
a pet dinosaur

00:53:14.091 --> 00:53:16.165
being talked into bed
and lambeosaurus is

00:53:16.166 --> 00:53:19.510
obviously about dinosaurs
but maybe we're

00:53:19.511 --> 00:53:22.960
talking about a different
register by lambeosaurus than,

00:53:22.961 --> 00:53:28.215
say, these imprisoned
or sleepy dinosaurs.

00:53:28.216 --> 00:53:31.580
So these are the sorts
of similarities and

00:53:31.581 --> 00:53:32.620
differences that
we're trying to tease

00:53:32.621 --> 00:53:34.560
out with the study.

00:53:34.561 --> 00:53:35.930
And so how might we

00:53:35.931 --> 00:53:37.725
do that and what kind
of data would we use?

00:53:37.726 --> 00:53:40.930
So what we use for our datum is,

00:53:40.931 --> 00:53:42.450
we use transcripts of

00:53:42.451 --> 00:53:44.700
the Autism Diagnostic
Observation Schedule

00:53:44.701 --> 00:53:47.440
or canonically ADOS.

00:53:47.441 --> 00:53:50.900
These are 60-minute language
samples from children with

00:53:50.901 --> 00:53:54.420
and without ASD diagnosis.

00:53:54.421 --> 00:53:57.290
And these are like

00:53:57.291 --> 00:54:00.110
a semi-structured task where

00:54:00.111 --> 00:54:01.450
there are certain questions

00:54:01.451 --> 00:54:03.140
that the examiner
is expected to ask,

00:54:03.141 --> 00:54:05.230
and there's certain activities

00:54:05.231 --> 00:54:06.590
that the child
typically engages with.

00:54:06.591 --> 00:54:07.805
But they're largely child driven,

00:54:07.806 --> 00:54:08.930
but they are crucially

00:54:08.931 --> 00:54:10.920
conversations with
an adult examiner.

00:54:10.921 --> 00:54:12.350
In our data, we're looking at

00:54:12.351 --> 00:54:14.495
children between the ages
of five and eight,

00:54:14.496 --> 00:54:17.390
and we have 32 children

00:54:17.391 --> 00:54:19.455
who've been diagnosed
with autism,

00:54:19.456 --> 00:54:23.790
38 who have, 32 have not
been diagnosed as autistic.

00:54:23.791 --> 00:54:27.680
So, what do we use
for Semantic model?

00:54:27.681 --> 00:54:28.400
If we're going to talk about what

00:54:28.401 --> 00:54:30.045
these children are talking about,

00:54:30.046 --> 00:54:31.550
and to be clear,

00:54:31.551 --> 00:54:34.975
those many transcripts were

00:54:34.976 --> 00:54:39.045
actually subsets of
actual ADOS that we have,

00:54:39.046 --> 00:54:41.870
there are children talking
about dinosaurs and hot sauce.

00:54:41.871 --> 00:54:44.410
So for this, it started as

00:54:44.411 --> 00:54:47.580
a replication experiment
following Goodkind et al,

00:54:47.581 --> 00:54:49.765
and they're a group in
North-Eastern University

00:54:49.766 --> 00:54:51.130
who found that using

00:54:51.131 --> 00:54:55.050
Word2Vec embeddings
and reference child

00:54:55.051 --> 00:54:56.600
selected from a pool of

00:54:56.601 --> 00:54:58.815
typically developing
children that they could

00:54:58.816 --> 00:55:01.835
find group differences
in this Word2Vec space

00:55:01.836 --> 00:55:03.520
between what the children
were talking about and

00:55:03.521 --> 00:55:05.470
we'll take a look at what that
may look like in a minute.

00:55:05.471 --> 00:55:07.235
So, similarly, we use

00:55:07.236 --> 00:55:09.990
the pre-trained Google
News Word2Vec embeddings.

00:55:09.991 --> 00:55:11.800
These are word embeddings

00:55:11.801 --> 00:55:13.450
for about three
million words that

00:55:13.451 --> 00:55:18.315
result in 300-dimension vectors.

00:55:18.316 --> 00:55:20.700
We then took, for
our transcripts,

00:55:20.701 --> 00:55:22.000
we took each word
in the transcript,

00:55:22.001 --> 00:55:24.665
projected that into
this Word2Vec vector space.

00:55:24.666 --> 00:55:28.230
Some of those vectors,

00:55:28.231 --> 00:55:32.295
and then normalize the resulting
vector into unit length,

00:55:32.296 --> 00:55:33.480
and call that our

00:55:33.481 --> 00:55:35.620
semantic representations
of the transcript.

00:55:35.621 --> 00:55:38.090
Then we'll use cosine similarity
in the space which is

00:55:38.091 --> 00:55:39.140
a measure of the angle between

00:55:39.141 --> 00:55:41.530
them to measure their similarity.

00:55:41.660 --> 00:55:45.525
All right, so how
similar are they?

00:55:45.526 --> 00:55:47.230
Let's imagine that we took

00:55:47.231 --> 00:55:48.700
the very first child who

00:55:48.701 --> 00:55:50.460
incidentally is
typically developing,

00:55:50.461 --> 00:55:55.500
and compared
the cosine similarity

00:55:55.501 --> 00:55:57.560
of all of the other
children with them,

00:55:57.561 --> 00:55:59.250
and then split them up by group,

00:55:59.251 --> 00:56:00.600
and this is what that
would look like,

00:56:00.601 --> 00:56:02.395
this is actually of that subject.

00:56:02.396 --> 00:56:04.740
For people who are familiar
with the North-Eastern paper,

00:56:04.741 --> 00:56:07.150
this is essentially
their very first result,

00:56:07.151 --> 00:56:09.165
and we were actually
able to replicate it.

00:56:09.166 --> 00:56:12.070
So, what you see is
that by and large,

00:56:12.071 --> 00:56:15.175
and this is the mean,
this dot here,

00:56:15.176 --> 00:56:17.020
the average similarity of

00:56:17.021 --> 00:56:18.545
typically developing children to

00:56:18.546 --> 00:56:23.580
this reference
transcript are more

00:56:23.581 --> 00:56:29.095
similar than the children
with ASD diagnosis.

00:56:29.096 --> 00:56:31.190
Also, you'll notice that there's

00:56:31.191 --> 00:56:34.100
more variability
around ASD subject as

00:56:34.101 --> 00:56:37.625
represented by a 95 percent
bootstrap confidence interval

00:56:37.626 --> 00:56:39.045
than there is on the TD kid,

00:56:39.046 --> 00:56:40.585
but maybe this kid,

00:56:40.586 --> 00:56:42.130
he admittedly like dinosaurs,

00:56:42.131 --> 00:56:44.500
maybe he had like that's
an idiosyncratic topic.

00:56:44.501 --> 00:56:46.840
So, let's choose
a couple of more kids,

00:56:46.841 --> 00:56:48.880
a couple more typically
developing kids,

00:56:48.881 --> 00:56:52.310
and see how whether we
still see the similarity.

00:56:52.840 --> 00:56:56.260
So, the one on the middle
is the one you just saw,

00:56:56.261 --> 00:56:59.080
and you'll notice that

00:56:59.081 --> 00:57:02.090
there's actually some
variability in the results based

00:57:02.091 --> 00:57:04.280
on who you choose
as your reference

00:57:04.281 --> 00:57:08.145
this here is the groups are
almost indistinguishable.

00:57:08.146 --> 00:57:09.560
But, by and large,

00:57:09.561 --> 00:57:11.550
we see that the typically
developing kids are on

00:57:11.551 --> 00:57:13.080
average more similar to

00:57:13.081 --> 00:57:15.215
the reference transcript
in their transcripts,

00:57:15.216 --> 00:57:18.595
than the children on
the ASD diagnosis.

00:57:18.596 --> 00:57:21.555
So, let's look at all the kids.

00:57:21.556 --> 00:57:25.110
So, the ticks on the x-axis here,

00:57:25.111 --> 00:57:28.150
these are all different
reference children,

00:57:28.151 --> 00:57:30.260
each represents the entire set

00:57:30.261 --> 00:57:34.135
of typically developing
children from our dataset,

00:57:34.136 --> 00:57:36.445
and we see that, what do we see,

00:57:36.446 --> 00:57:39.510
well I actually have
slides for that.

00:57:39.511 --> 00:57:41.840
So again,

00:57:41.841 --> 00:57:43.630
we see the language of
typically developing children

00:57:43.631 --> 00:57:44.610
seen as more similar to

00:57:44.611 --> 00:57:46.070
any typically
developing transcript

00:57:46.071 --> 00:57:47.475
than those of

00:57:47.476 --> 00:57:49.250
those transcripts of
the children with

00:57:49.251 --> 00:57:52.880
autism spectrum
disorder diagnosis.

00:57:53.860 --> 00:57:56.200
For every case, we see

00:57:56.201 --> 00:57:57.230
more variance across the

00:57:57.231 --> 00:57:58.460
similarity scores
for children with

00:57:58.461 --> 00:58:01.010
ASD than the typically
developing children,

00:58:01.011 --> 00:58:02.865
but we do get these outliers.

00:58:02.866 --> 00:58:04.625
However, despite the variability,

00:58:04.626 --> 00:58:06.090
the pattern is pretty

00:58:06.091 --> 00:58:08.370
stable that the blue dots
are above that red dots,

00:58:08.371 --> 00:58:10.820
and that the variant scores or

00:58:10.821 --> 00:58:14.385
the variance ranges are
larger for the red points.

00:58:14.386 --> 00:58:17.145
So, what does this mean?

00:58:17.146 --> 00:58:20.805
Also, following
the North-Eastern study,

00:58:20.806 --> 00:58:22.060
yeah, in most cases here,

00:58:22.061 --> 00:58:24.495
the differences is enough to
determine group difference.

00:58:24.496 --> 00:58:25.930
So, it does make sense that

00:58:25.931 --> 00:58:27.900
their choice for
reference document

00:58:27.901 --> 00:58:29.830
from a typically
developing children would

00:58:29.831 --> 00:58:31.005
result in something like this,

00:58:31.006 --> 00:58:32.390
at least in our data as well.

00:58:32.391 --> 00:58:34.925
So, what does it mean?

00:58:34.926 --> 00:58:36.560
It can mean a number of things.

00:58:36.561 --> 00:58:38.845
The first thing to remember

00:58:38.846 --> 00:58:42.830
is that these samples aren't
from creating a vacuum.

00:58:42.831 --> 00:58:47.315
These are from a conversation
with the examiner,

00:58:47.316 --> 00:58:52.430
and it's possible
that the space we see

00:58:52.431 --> 00:58:54.900
between the two is
just an artifact of that

00:58:54.901 --> 00:58:56.240
maybe that the typically
developing children

00:58:56.241 --> 00:58:58.415
are more influenced
by the examiner.

00:58:58.416 --> 00:59:00.920
It may also be that

00:59:00.921 --> 00:59:03.550
the children who have
ASD talked about

00:59:03.551 --> 00:59:07.650
more idiosyncratic topics
but it could also be

00:59:07.651 --> 00:59:09.125
other things as well maybe

00:59:09.126 --> 00:59:11.760
all typically developing
children talk about dinosaurs,

00:59:11.761 --> 00:59:13.340
and all like childred

00:59:13.341 --> 00:59:14.410
with ASD talk about
something that's

00:59:14.411 --> 00:59:17.770
not dinosaurs that seems
exceptionally unlikely.

00:59:17.771 --> 00:59:20.060
But, it could also
be that children

00:59:20.061 --> 00:59:22.150
with ASD are just more
linguistically creative,

00:59:22.151 --> 00:59:23.790
and that's what's
being picked up here.

00:59:23.791 --> 00:59:26.795
So, this is what we're
interested for future work.

00:59:26.796 --> 00:59:28.520
So, the first thing we're

00:59:28.521 --> 00:59:31.810
doing is in all of
the previous slides,

00:59:31.811 --> 00:59:34.290
the data that you saw, all

00:59:34.291 --> 00:59:36.220
of the girls had been
excised from our data,

00:59:36.221 --> 00:59:39.910
so we're just looking at
children who are boys,

00:59:40.250 --> 00:59:44.190
largely because it's just
hard to find transcripts of,

00:59:44.191 --> 00:59:47.970
in bulk, of girls with autism.

00:59:47.971 --> 00:59:51.470
So, we have an extension
to one of our grants to

00:59:51.471 --> 00:59:53.650
collect the transcripts
of [inaudible]

00:59:53.651 --> 00:59:55.945
with girls with ASD diagnosis.

00:59:55.946 --> 00:59:57.160
Its work here is to
see whether we see

00:59:57.161 --> 00:59:59.740
a similar pattern
in speech of girls.

01:00:00.130 --> 01:00:02.860
We're also, as we discussed
in the previous slide,

01:00:02.861 --> 01:00:05.750
interested in studying how much

01:00:05.751 --> 01:00:10.485
the language in
semantic or topic space

01:00:10.486 --> 01:00:12.600
of the child either

01:00:12.601 --> 01:00:15.100
leads or follows that
of the examiner.

01:00:15.101 --> 01:00:18.310
Also, do certain conversational
context because keep

01:00:18.311 --> 01:00:20.835
in mind the ADOS has
different types of activities,

01:00:20.836 --> 01:00:22.690
do those kind of contexts affect

01:00:22.691 --> 01:00:25.385
the amount of variability
in children's speech.

01:00:25.386 --> 01:00:27.575
This one is maybe obvious, but

01:00:27.576 --> 01:00:29.270
obviously another future step is

01:00:29.271 --> 01:00:34.270
that representing
our fairly large transcripts

01:00:34.271 --> 01:00:37.130
by just summing all of
the word to vec vectors,

01:00:37.131 --> 01:00:40.120
and then reducing them

01:00:40.121 --> 01:00:42.225
down to unit length
is a little course.

01:00:42.226 --> 01:00:43.780
So, we'd like to improve

01:00:43.781 --> 01:00:47.950
the document semantic
representations as well.

01:00:47.951 --> 01:00:51.290
So, in closing I'd like
to thank my co-authors.

01:00:51.291 --> 01:00:52.860
Alexandra Salem who's here,

01:00:52.861 --> 01:00:55.365
and doctors Alison Hill,

01:00:55.366 --> 01:00:57.570
Steven Bedrick, and
Jan van Santen,

01:00:57.571 --> 01:00:59.160
and if there are any questions,

01:00:59.161 --> 01:01:09.330
I'd love to hear them.

01:01:09.331 --> 01:01:11.110
&gt;&gt; Hi, great talk. Have you tried

01:01:11.111 --> 01:01:12.850
comparing the semantic similarity

01:01:12.851 --> 01:01:14.540
of your data to

01:01:14.541 --> 01:01:17.170
a reference speakers with
on the autism spectrum?

01:01:17.171 --> 01:01:18.505
&gt;&gt; I'm sorry. What was that?

01:01:18.506 --> 01:01:21.115
&gt;&gt; Have you tried comparing
the semantic similarity of

01:01:21.116 --> 01:01:23.410
your speakers to
a reference speaker

01:01:23.411 --> 01:01:24.550
on the autism spectrum?

01:01:24.551 --> 01:01:27.150
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's actually
a really good question.

01:01:27.151 --> 01:01:28.790
I was thinking as
I drove up here,

01:01:28.791 --> 01:01:30.780
that I might- the pleasure

01:01:30.781 --> 01:01:32.425
of including that as
like a bonus slide.

01:01:32.426 --> 01:01:36.680
But, yeah, I've done

01:01:36.681 --> 01:01:39.240
the exact same thought
with instead of

01:01:39.241 --> 01:01:40.990
the typically
developing children as

01:01:40.991 --> 01:01:42.885
a reference documents
using the ASD ones.

01:01:42.886 --> 01:01:48.300
There's a lot more swapping

01:01:48.301 --> 01:01:51.560
of the positions of the
red, and blue dots.

01:01:51.790 --> 01:01:54.740
In a lot of cases,

01:01:54.741 --> 01:01:56.110
the differentiation between

01:01:56.111 --> 01:01:58.760
the two diagnosis groups are very

01:01:58.761 --> 01:02:04.200
challenging using the ASD
children as the reference.

01:02:06.790 --> 01:02:09.594
&gt;&gt; Sir, in your analysis,

01:02:09.595 --> 01:02:13.335
did you consider where on
the spectrum the children were?

01:02:13.336 --> 01:02:15.090
&gt;&gt; That's a really good question.

01:02:15.091 --> 01:02:17.710
We did not for this.

01:02:17.711 --> 01:02:21.400
One of my advisers,
Jan van Santen,

01:02:21.401 --> 01:02:25.575
actually asked me specifically
to consider that as maybe,

01:02:25.576 --> 01:02:27.890
a covariate here is
like the, for example,

01:02:27.891 --> 01:02:29.820
ADOS score, for example,

01:02:29.821 --> 01:02:32.080
but we have not done that yet.

01:02:33.880 --> 01:02:36.620
&gt;&gt; Time for one more.

01:02:36.621 --> 01:02:41.200
&gt;&gt; Do you have

01:02:41.201 --> 01:02:45.735
any ideas of what how this
might map onto clinical work,

01:02:45.736 --> 01:02:47.235
if it's going to help us,

01:02:47.236 --> 01:02:50.010
maybe pre ADOS collect,

01:02:50.011 --> 01:02:52.760
who may be at risk for autism or

01:02:52.761 --> 01:02:55.165
help us decide on
these difficult cases?

01:02:55.166 --> 01:02:58.050
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, that's

01:02:58.051 --> 01:02:59.640
a really good question
like how is this

01:02:59.641 --> 01:03:01.990
like is there a clinical
utility to this.

01:03:01.991 --> 01:03:04.290
I think that's always
the first question that

01:03:04.291 --> 01:03:07.580
we asked ourselves when
we do this research.

01:03:09.190 --> 01:03:11.460
So, for example, like

01:03:11.461 --> 01:03:14.590
one thing that may fall
out of this is maybe

01:03:14.591 --> 01:03:18.520
certain aspects of
the ADOS proved to be

01:03:18.521 --> 01:03:22.470
better at showing this kind

01:03:22.471 --> 01:03:23.575
of semantic difference
than others.

01:03:23.576 --> 01:03:25.320
For example, it looks
like cases where

01:03:25.321 --> 01:03:30.980
the conversation more structured

01:03:30.981 --> 01:03:34.765
like the question section
of the ADOS is a lot more,

01:03:34.766 --> 01:03:36.550
you see a lot less variability

01:03:36.551 --> 01:03:38.260
about what the typically
developing kids are,

01:03:38.261 --> 01:03:41.640
a lot more variability
with the ASD kids are,

01:03:42.790 --> 01:03:47.465
relatively in a lot more
group differentiation.

01:03:47.466 --> 01:03:49.170
So if, perhaps there's

01:03:49.171 --> 01:03:50.740
a section of the ADOS is
more important than those,

01:03:50.741 --> 01:03:52.940
that seems like it
might be for this kind

01:03:52.941 --> 01:03:55.445
of semantic similarity measure,

01:03:55.446 --> 01:03:58.680
that might be useful
in the long run.

01:04:00.550 --> 01:04:03.180
We're actually looking for

01:04:03.181 --> 01:04:04.730
more than group differentiations,

01:04:04.731 --> 01:04:08.820
we're more interested in using

01:04:08.821 --> 01:04:11.230
these measures of variation

01:04:11.231 --> 01:04:14.015
in language as
longitudinal measures,

01:04:14.016 --> 01:04:16.315
like that's what started
this [inaudible].

01:04:16.316 --> 01:04:20.140
So, how much does this change
if we've switched children,

01:04:20.141 --> 01:04:23.020
but maybe also like how much
does this measure change

01:04:23.021 --> 01:04:24.240
if we measure it over time.

01:04:24.241 --> 01:04:26.570
So, those sort of things we're
currently thinking about.

01:04:26.571 --> 01:04:29.260
But, yeah, we're early
on that stage for sure.

01:04:29.261 --> 01:04:32.210
&gt;&gt; All right, let's
thank our speaker,

01:04:33.280 --> 01:04:37.220
and thanks to all of

01:04:37.221 --> 01:04:38.990
our short paper presenters

01:04:38.991 --> 01:04:40.390
for getting a whole lot
of information,

01:04:40.391 --> 01:04:41.700
and not a whole lot
of [inaudible].

01:04:41.701 --> 01:04:43.950
&gt;&gt; That was amazing.

